Luminescent metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been received much attention due to their widely structural tunability and potential applications in light-emitting diodes, biological images and chemical sensors. However, successful examples on long-persistent afterglow MOFs are still quite limited to date. In this work, we report two types of Znterephthalate (TPA) MOFs (namely [Zn(TPA)(DMF)] (1-DMF) and MOF-5) could exhibit an obvious room-temperature afterglow emission with the time-resolved luminescence lifetime as high as 0.47 second. The phosphorescence-based afterglow was also highly sensitive to the temperature, and the reversible emission intensity can be recycled under high/low temperatures. Moreover, both 1-DMF and MOF-5 showed highly tunable afterglow phosphorescence colors (from cyan to yellow and from green to red respectively) upon treated with pyridine solution. The fluorescence/phosphorescence emission color of MOF-5 can be reversibly switched due to the addition and removal of pyridine guest from the host nanochannel, as shown in both experimental and computational studies. Therefore, this work not only paves a facile way to develop MOF-based long-afterglow materials at room temperature, but also present a strategy to tune their phosphorescence in a wide range based on host-guest interactions.
## Introduction
Afterglow, long-lasting phosphorescence or persistent luminescence, which can last for an appreciable time after the removal of the excitation source, have attracted great attention during last few decades,1 owing to their commercial applications in the fields of traffic signs, interior decoration, emergent-lighting and display, as well as potential uses as chemical sensing, optical recording devices, biological imaging, and security systems.3 Since Matsuzawa et al. reported the long-lasting afterglow of $\mathsf{S r A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\colon\mathsf{E u}^{2+}$ co-doped with ${\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{y}}^{3+}$ ions in 1996,4 research on efficient persistent phosphors has continuously gained much interest. However, the number and type of persistent luminescent materials are still relatively limited to date, and the interests worldwide have mainly focused on rare-earth (RE)-containing inorganic materials.5 Unfortunately, the high-cost and relative complicated preparation methods (such as high temperature solid-state process) present a barrier to commercialization.6 Therefore, the development of new types of inexpensive, energy-efficient, eco-friendly, and RE-free afterglow materials is highly desirable.
Combination of inorganic unit and organic link, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have received much attention recently from both fundamental research and industrial fields for their applications as gas storage/separation,7 catalysis,8 drug delivery,9 and energy storage and conversion.10 Moreover, luminescent MOFs have also been extensively studied owing to their facile synthesis routes, tunable structures and applications in light-emitting diodes,11 biological images12 and chemical sensors.13 Luminescent MOFs14 containing noble-metal (NM, such as Pt, Ir and Ru) and RE (such as Eu, Tb) usually present the photoemission lifetimes in the range from hundreds of microseconds $(\upmu\mathsf{s})$ to few milliseconds (ms) based on the ligand-metal or metal-ligand charge transfer (LMCT/MLCT) mechanism. However, the relative low luminescence decay time cannot meet the requirement of persistent luminescence application, since the recognition of afterglow emission for the common human generally requires the emission decay at tens of ms level. Recently, Sun et al have reported that a Cd-based MOF could exhibit afterglow behavior only under lowtemperature (77K) condition;15 therefore, how to obtain longafterglow phosphors at ambient condition based on the facile design of MOFs is continues a challenging goal.
On the other hand, MOFs with dynamic frameworks can reversibly change their structures and significantly improve the desirable properties as smart material applications.16 Typically, dynamic MOFs involve a guest-induced transformation between two or more structures upon solvation and/or guest adsorption.17 In some cases, the guest-induced structural change can result in a sensory response (such as fluorescence18 and magnetism19). Although much effort has been devoted to these dynamic MOFbased materials,20 the host-guest systems combining both tunable long-lasting afterglow and smart MOF sensor has rarely been reported (Scheme 1). Herein, we report two types of Znterephthalate (TPA) MOFs (namely [Zn(TPA)(DMF)] (1-DMF) and MOF-5) could exhibit an obvious room-temperature afterglow emission with the time-resolved luminescence lifetime as high as 0.47 second. Moreover, the as-obtained MOF samples could present highly tunable phosphorescence color (from cyan to yellow and from green to red, respectively) based on the introduction of guest molecules into the MOF nanochannels. Periodic density functional theoretical calculation showed that the guest molecules can largely regulate the frontier orbital and electronic structures of the pristine MOF host structure. Therefore, this work supplies a cost-effective way to obtain high-performance long-afterglow MOFs at room temperature, which could further serve as potential heat and solvent phosphorescence sensor.
## Results and Discussion
Interestingly, the crystalline 1-DMF and MOF-5 present the fluorescence emission at 326 and $434~\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 2a and 2b) with fluorescence quantum yield of $2.25\%$ and $46.27\%$ , respectively. The corresponding crystal images under UV and daylight were shown in the insets. Under ambient condition, the phosphorescence emission at 503 and $513\ \mathsf{n m}$ for 1-DMF and MOF-5 were detected, with phosphorescence quantum yield of $4.76\%$ and $0.22\%$ , respectively. The difference in photoemission properties can be ascribed to the different stacking fashions of the ligands and different Zn-based metal clusters between two MOFs. In addition, the coordinated DMF in 1-DMF may also influence the photophysical process. Moreover, the phosphorescence decay curve gave their phosphorescence lifetime values of ca. 472 and $153\quad{\mathrm{ms}},$ respectively (Fig. 2c). Particularly, after removal of the UV light, green emission could be recognized easily by naked eye, and also captured by a digital camera in the time range 0–4 seconds, confirming that the long-afterglow characteristics for the Zn-based MOFs (Fig 2d).
(d) Photographs of the afterglow for 1-DMF taken at different time intervals before and after turning off the UV excitation $(365~\mathsf{n m})$ under ambient conditions. Photographs of crystal images under daylight and UV are shown in the insets.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) confirmed the highly purity of both 1-DMF and MOF-5 samples (Fig. S3), and thus excluded any emission from pristine TPA crystals. It was reported that the pure TPA present very weak phosphorescence with the emission lifetime of ca. $0.5~\mathsf{m s},^{22}$ and thus the lifetime value has been enhanced nearly three orders of magnitude upon the formation of MOFs structures, relative to the pure ligands. Moreover, compared with the classical RE-doped afterglow phosphors (such as $\mathsf{S r A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\colon\mathsf{E u}^{2+}/\mathsf{D y}^{3+})$ with the same emission color before and after turning off UV source, the two MOFs samples in this work present obviously changeable emissive colors due to the fluorescence/phosphorescence transformation. Such timedependent afterglow change may supply the MOF systems an opportunity for potential optical antiforgery application.
To better understand the long-persistent afterglow as well as the electronic structure of 1-DMF, period density functional theoretical (PDFT) calculations were performed (Fig. 3 and Fig. S4). Total/partial electronic density of state (TDOS and PDOS) showed that the 1- DMF present a low band gap (3.31 eV, $375\ n m_{\cdot}^{\prime}$ ), which may corresponds to the fluorescence emission around $326~\mathsf{n m}$ . Around the Fermi level, the TDOS mainly consist of the $2p$ electrons of O atoms from TPA ligand and $d$ electrons from Zn atoms. Frontier orbital analysis shows that the electron-density distribution of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) is mainly located on $Z n_{2}O_{8}$ metal nanoclusters containing $Z n(11)$ ions and O atoms in TPA ligands, the lowest singlet excitation is governed by the transition from HOMO to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO). The electron-density distribution of LUMO and $\mathsf{L U M O}{+}1$ is dominated by TPA ligands. Therefore, the photo-excitation and emission involves an MLCT process from $Z n_{2}O_{8}$ units to the organic links, which usually present a prolonged excitation lifetime. Combination of both experimental and computational results, it can be concluded that the long-afterglow of the 1-DMF sample can be attributed to: (1) the $Z n(11)$ ion has participated into the frontier orbital distributions and thus may promote spin–orbit coupling and improve electron-hole separation time during the charge transfer process; (2) the molecular vibrations and nonradiative loss can be highly reduced based on the coordination interactions between $Z n(11)$ and TPA, since the organic ligands are tightly fixed and locked in the rigid framework. This may further increase the intersystem crossing between the S1 and T1 excited states.23
Thermal behavior is important to understand the coordination states for the MOF phosphors. Thermogravimetric analyses (Fig. S5a) shows that 1-DMF is stable up to about $130^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , and the weight loss of $24.3\%$ in the temperature range $130-330^{\circ}C$ corresponds to the release of coordinated DMF molecules (calc. $24.5\%$ . Above $380^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ 1-DMF begins to decompose due to the burning of the TPA units. In the case of MOF-5 (Fig. S5b), the weight loses of guest molecules are in the temperature range of 80 to $220^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and the resulting porous framework starts to decompose after $410^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Furthermore, to detect the heat-related persistent afterglow, temperaturedependent phosphorescence spectra of the 1-DMF crystal were further measured. It was observed that the bands at $503~\mathsf{n m}$ were decreased obviously upon the increase of the temperature from ambient condition (293 K) to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 4a). The corresponding phosphorescence lifetime also decreased with the value of $106~\mathsf{m s}$ at 373 K (Fig. 4b). Similar to other long-afterglow materials, this behavior is related to the fact that the molecular vibrations and nonradiative loss highly increased under high temperature. Moreover, if 1-DMF was cooled down to $293\mathsf{K},$ it was observed that the phosphorescence emissive intensity can nearly recover its original position, and the reversible change in luminescence can be readily repeated at least for 4 times during such heating-cooling cycles (Fig. 4c). Therefore, the 1-DMF can potentially serve as a phosphorescence sensor towards heat detector.
Open coordination sites (OCSs) often play a key role in functional MOFs, such as gas separation/sorption and catalysis.24 Recently, it has been shown that Zn-based paddle wheel SBUs present a dynamic structural transformation upon the removal and rebinding of guest molecules.17,21 In the network of 1-DMF of this work, large channels may allow fast access of guests through their large open spaces. In addition, the coordinated DMF molecules are also exposed in the MOF nanochannel. With the development of potential afterglow-based sensor in mind, the potential solvatochromic emission behavior of 1-DMF was detected by measuring the luminescence of the samples upon treatment with different solvents (Fig S6). It was observed that the phosphorescence quenching occurred to different extents after solvents treatment. Specifically, the phosphorescence intensity of 1-DMF decreases obviously after treated with MeOH and acetone, suggesting that the 1-DMF may serve as the phosphorescent sensor for MeOH and acetone through phosphorescence quenching (Fig S6). This observation may be related to the substitution of the coordinated DMF by other solvents, which results in the alternation of the pristine crystal structure and related photophysical properties. This can be further confirmed by the change of the PXRD patterns before and after solvents treatment (Fig. S7). Meanwhile, the wavelength shifts of the phosphorescence emission have been observed after treated with water and pyridine solvents. For example, after soaking the 1-DMF in water (1-Water), the sample undergoes a phosphorescence red-shift of ca. $6\mathsf{n m}$ ; further PXRD measurement showed that the 1-Water system present an obvious structural difference compared with the 1-DMF. However, the 1-Water exhibit an isostructuralism with MOF-2,25 indicating a crystal-to-crystal transformation occurs during the water treatment (Fig S7 and Table S3). Thus, the overall framework connectivity in 1- Water has maintained, while the replacement of the DMF ligand by the water molecules has shortened the nearest interlayer $z_{n}...z_{n}$ separation from 6.44 to $4.83\mathrm{~\AA},$ giving rise to a slight volume decrease. This is owing to the smaller steric requirement of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ compared with DMF. Meanwhile, the increase in the distance between two neighboring paddle-wheel units in the layer can also be observed (Fig. S8).
More interestingly, when 1-DMF was treated with pyridine (PY) to obtain 1-PY solid sample, there is an obvious luminescence changes for their fluorescence and phosphorescence located at 440 and $570~\mathsf{n m}$ $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=350~\mathrm{nm}.$ , Fig. 5a), with the maximum wavelength shifts of $114\ \mathsf{n m}$ and 67 nm respectively. The phosphorescence emission undergoes a remarkable transformation from cyan (color coordination: (0.178, 0.440)) to yellow color (color coordination: (0.439, 0.549)) as shown in Fig. 5c, and the corresponding phosphorescence lifetime has also reduced to ca. $30~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{s}$ (Fig. 5b).
The PXRD studies showed that the structure of 1-PY is also similar to MOF-2 (Fig S7), and thus it can be speculated that the coordinated DMF units has been replaced by the PY.
To better understand the notable emission shifts for the 1-PY compared with 1-DMF, we further carried out PDFT calculation on the idealized 1-PY model, in which half of DMF molecules have been substituted by coordinated PY (Fig. 6). Frontier orbital analysis shows that the electron-density of HOMO and HOMO‒1 is mainly located on $Z n_{2}O_{8}$ metal cluster containing $Z n(11)$ ions and O atoms in TPA ligands (Fig 6a and 6b), while the electron-density distribution of LUMO and $\mathsf{L U M O}+1$ is mainly populated on the both TPA and PY (Fig 6c and 6d). The residual DMF molecules have not contributed to the frontier orbital distribution. Moreover, band structure analysis showed that the 1-PY present a reduced band gap of 3.02 eV (ca. $411{\mathsf{n m}}$ ), which could rationally correspond to the emission red-shift in experiment. Therefore, it can be concluded that the coordinated PY has participated in the photoemisson process, and the introduction of intermediate energy levels has further shortened the band gap, which could result in the wavelength redshift for both fluorescence and phosphorescence. Furthermore, the occurrence of energy/electronic transfer from MOF host to PY guest could also result in a loss of triplet energy in the host framework, and thus leads to a reduced phosphorescence lifetime.
Inspired by the highly tunable phosphorescence color from 1- DMF to 1-PY system, the MOF-5 crystal has been further soaked with PY solvent. Upon excitation at $350~\mathsf{n m}$ under ambient condition, we observed that MOF-5 also underwent obvious luminescence changes with the fluorescence and phosphorescence emission located at $564~\mathsf{n m}$ (yellow color) and $650~\mathsf{n m}$ (red color), respectively (Fig 7a). The PXRD profiles showed that the pattern of MOF-5 has nearly unchanged before and after treated with PY, suggesting that the host framework has maintained (Fig S9). UV/Vis spectra (Fig. S10) show that the optical absorption characteristics of the 1-DMF, MOF-5 and PY@1-DMF present very similar absorption positions below $310\mathsf{n m}$ . While for the PY@MOF-5 sample, two new absorption peaks (353 and $420~\mathsf{n m})$ appeared, which can be assigned to introduction of intermediate energy levels from the PY guest molecules, as also confirmed by theoretical calculation below.
Considering that the MOF-5 present a relatively large channel (size: $1.1\mathsf{n m}\times1.1\mathsf{n m}_{.}^{}$ ) without additional coordination sites, the PY molecules can be potentially as guests into the nanochannel based on $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions with TPA ligands. Therefore, the color-changed PY@MOF-5 sample has been further heated to remove the interchannel PY. In contrast to the 1-PY, the fluorescence and phosphorescence of the PY@MOF-5 can be easily recovered after heating the sample at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 6 hours, which further confirms the guest-induced emission change for the PY@MOF-5 system. Moreover, the addition and removal of the PY molecules can be recycled by alternatively treated with PY and high-temperature conditions for at least three times, as confirmed by the reversible luminescent transformation between long/short wavelengths (Fig 7b and 7c).
To further understand how the introduction of the PY guest can induce the phosphorescence change from green to red color, PDFT was further performed on the PY@MOF-5 host-guest system, in which three PY guest molecules were selected as a model to assemble within one nanochannel of MOF-5 uniformly (Fig 8). Energy level analysis shows that the introduction of PY has highly reduced the band gap $(2.17\ \mathrm{eV})$ , compared with the pristine MOF-5 (ca. 3.4 eV). The HOMO is mainly contributed from the PY molecules, and the HOMO-1, LUMO, and $\mathsf{L U M O}{+}1$ are located within the MOF-5 host framework, confirming the PY can largely influence the frontier orbitals distributions in the host-guest system due to the introduction of intermediate energy levels. Therefore, it can be concluded that the strategy based on the change of hostguest interaction as well as guest-induced structural transformation can largely tune the phosphorescence emission in a wide range.
## Conclusions
In summary, we have confirmed that MOFs can serve as one new type of RE-free and NM-free room-temperature long-afterglow photoemission materials towards potential illumination and lightemitting applications. The key to the formation of afterglow MOFs at room temperature can be related to the selection of suitable phosphor ligands and metal ions with strong coordination interaction and potential charge transfer process. The as-prepared [Zn(TPA)(DMF)] MOF also present a reversible temperaturesensitive phosphorescence emission. Moreover, due to the dynamic tunabilities on both structures and host-guest interactions, a wide range phosphorescence color changes from cyan to yellow or from green to red can be achieved. Based on the selective phosphorescence wavelength shift upon treated by guest solvents, the MOFs can serve as a potential sensor towards pyridine detection. By virtue of both experimental and theoretical studies on the host-guest phosphorescence MOF systems, this study not only presents a feasible method for fabricating MOF-based afterglow materials with tunable phosphorescence, but also gives detailed understanding on the geometric/electronic structures of the guest molecules within the MOF matrix. It can be expected that the longafterglow MOFs can be extended to other systems based on their facile design, cost-effective solution process and controllable synthesis.
## Experimental Section
Reagents and Materials.
Analytically pure $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O,$ terephthalic acid (TPA), and $\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ - dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Sigma Chemical. Co. Ltd. and used without further purification.
Synthesis of 1-DMF.
A mixture of TPA $\cdot166~\mathsf{m g})$ , and $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ $\cdot595\mathrm{mg})$ was added to DMF $(10~\mathsf{m L})$ in a $25~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel vessel. The mixture was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ , and then slowly cooled down to the room temperature. A large quantity of block crystals [Zn(TPA)(DMF)] (1-DMF) were obtained, which were washed with DMF for several times under ambient conditions. $\mathsf{I R}/\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (KBr): 3428(m, br), 2930 (m), 1653(s), 1550(s), 1496(m), 1435 $(\mathsf{m})$ , 1386(s), $1104(\mathsf{m}),839(\mathsf{s}),750(\mathsf{s}),677(\mathsf{m}).$
Synthesis of MOF-5.
TPA $\dot{2}66\mathrm{mg})$ was dissolved in DMF $\{15~\mathsf{m L}\}$ ) in a $25~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel vessel, and the solution was sonicated for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ $\lvert595\mathrm{mg}\rvert$ ) was added to the above solution. The clear solution was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ , and then slowly cooled down to the room temperature. A large quantity of cubic block crystals MOF-5 were obtained, which were washed with DMF for several times under ambient conditions. $\mathsf{I R}/\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (KBr): 3455(w, br), 2957 (w), 1652(s), 1601(s),1580(s), 1388(s), 1104(m), 825(w), 752(s).
Characterization:
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction data of all compounds was collected on a Bruker SMART APEX CCD diffractometer26 equipped with graphite monochromatized Mo- $\mathsf{K a}$ radiation $(\lambda\:=\:0.71073\mathrm{~\AA})$ at room temperature using the $\upomega$ -scan technique. Empirical absorption corrections were applied to the intensities using the SADABS program.27 The structures were solved using the program SHELXS- $\cdot97^{28}$ and refined with the program SHELXL-97.29 All nonhydrogen atoms were refined anisotropically. The crystallographic data for 1-DMF are listed in Table S1 and Table S2. Crystallographic data for 1-DMF in this paper have also been deposited with the CCDC as deposition no. 1450104 (available free of charge, on application to the CCDC, 12 Union Rd., Cambridge CB2 1EZ, U.K.; e-mail deposit@ccdc.cam.ac.uk). PXRD patterns of all compounds were collected on a Rigaku Ultima-IV automated diffraction system with Cu $\upkappa\upalpha$ radiation $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\lambda}&{{}=}&{1.5406\mathrm{~\normalfont~\AA~}}\end{array}$ . Measurements were made in a 2θ range of $5\mathrm{-}50^{\circ}$ at room temperature with a step of $0.02^{\circ}$ (2θ) and a counting time of $0.2\mathrm{~s~/~}$ step. The operating power was 40 KV, $30~\mathsf{m A}$ . IR spectra were recorded in the range of $4000{\cdot}400~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ on a Tensor 27 OPUS (Bruker) FT-IR spectrometer with KBr pellets. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) experiments were carried out on a Perkin-Elmer Diamond SII thermal analyzer from room temperature to $800~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under nitrogen atmosphere at a heating rate of $10\mathrm{^{~\circ}C/m i n}$ Photoluminescence (PL) spectra, and time-resolved PL spectra experiments were conducted on an Edinburgh FLS980 fluorescence spectrometer. The excitation source for the time-resolved lifetime measurement is a microsecond flashlamp with time-resolved single photon counting‒multi-channel scaling (MCS) mode. The solid UV−vis absorp\`on spectra were collected on a Shimadzu $440\ \mathsf{U}\cdot$ 3000 spectrophotometer. The PL quantum yield (PLQY) values at room temperature were estimated using a Teflon-lined integrating sphere (F-M101, Edinburgh, diameter: $150~\mathsf{m m}$ and weight: $2\:\mathsf{k g}\backslash$ in FLS980 fluorescence spectrometer. The temperature dependence of the phosphorescence intensity, and phosphorescence lifetime were measured on a temperature controller attached to a cryostat (Oxford Ltd. Optistat DN2) in FLS980 fluorescence spectrometer. Photographs of single crystals for MOFs were taken under OLYMPUS IXTI fluorescence microscope. Photographs of the afterglow for 1-DMF taken at different time intervals before and after turning off the UV excitation $(365~\mathsf{n m})$ ) under ambient condition were captured by a Canon digital camera (EOS 700D: the ISO value was 1600, time of exposure was set in an automatic mode, and the value of aperture was 4.5).
Electronic structure calculations of selected Zn-based MOFs.
All calculations were performed with the periodic density functional theory (DFT) method using Dmol330a,b module in Material Studio software package.30c The initial configuration was fully optimized by Perdew-Wang (PW91)30d generalized gradient approximation (GGA) method with the double numerical basis sets plus polarization function (DNP). The core electrons for metals were treated by effective core potentials (ECP). The self-consistent field (SCF) converged criterion was within $1.0\times{10}^{-5}$ hartree atom-1 and the converging criterion of the structure optimization was $1.0\times{{10}^{-3}}$ hartree bohr-1. The Brillouin zone is sampled by $1\times1\times1\ k$ -points, and test calculations reveal that the increase of $k$ -points does not affect the results.
